Rearrange the equation to the standard form of a Linear Differential Equation (L.D.E), \(\frac{dy}{dx} + P(x)y = Q(x)\):
\frac{dy}{dx} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}y = e^{x-2\sqrt{x}-2}
Here, \(P(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\) and \(Q(x) = e^{x-2\sqrt{x}-2}\).
Calculate the Integrating Factor (I.F.):
\text{I.F.} = e^{\int P(x)dx} = e^{\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}dx} = e^{\int x^{-1/2}dx} = e^{2x^{1/2}} = e^{2\sqrt{x}}
The general solution of a L.D.E is given by \(y \times \text{I.F.} = \int Q(x) \times \text{I.F.} dx + c\):
y \times e^{2\sqrt{x}} = \int e^{x-2\sqrt{x}-2} \times e^{2\sqrt{x}} dx + c
y \times e^{2\sqrt{x}} = \int e^{x-2} dx + c
y \times e^{2\sqrt{x}} = e^{x-2} + c
Given the initial condition \(y(0) = 0\). Substitute \(x = 0\) and \(y = 0\) into the solution to find \(c\):
0 \times e^{2\sqrt{0}} = e^{0-2} + c
0 = e^{-2} + c \implies c = -e^{-2}
Substitute the value of \(c\) back into the general solution:
